ce-book-text-pantheon-gods-sileita-imitators = [head=2]Imitators of the Gods[/head]

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]Echo-Spirits, Mask-Bearers, Pseudo-Divines.[/italic]

    Imitators are beings, spirits, phantoms or possessed entities that take on the appearance, speech, mannerisms and goals of one of the gods, without possessing their blessing or sanction. They are not true servants, but may outwardly copy divine manifestations indistinguishably. This phenomenon occurs both in ritual art and the magic of persuasion, and in unconscious forms of distorted faith. There are also unique beings created unconsciously, under the influence of magical anomalies, egregors (collective consciousness) of faith, or the Void.

    {"[head=2]General Nature of Imitators[/head]"}

    They do not receive power from the god itself, but may use rituals that mimic miracles and/or the god's energy.
    Often appear in places of strong religious tension — in abandoned temples, on battlefields where gods were worshipped, or in regions forgotten by the priesthood.
    Some Imitators are spirits born from the echo of collective faith that have absorbed the energy of the gods; others are mortals consumed by an idea.
    Sometimes a god knows of the imitator but does not react — considering it 'noise of faith'.

    {"[head=2]Typology of Imitators[/head]"}

    {"[head=3][color=goldenrod]Shadow Faces[/color][/head]"}

    Beings that copy the appearance and mannerisms of the gods. Often these are spirits that, at the moment of evolution, were saturated with the energy of the gods, or lived at that time in places of religious disturbance.
    {"[italic]Example[/italic]: A spirit dwelling by a goddess's altar adopts her appearance and mannerisms, becoming a weak copy."}

    {"[head=3][color=goldenrod]Echo Spirits[/color][/head]"}

    Incorporeal formations arising from an excess of prayers or fear. They have no will, but reflect a specific aspect of a god — Jelarion's wrath, Arkhofeya's compassion, etc. They may 'infect' people, making them temporary carriers of a behavioral pattern. Often appear near children who have experienced religious trauma or ecstasy.
    {"[italic]Example[/italic]: a silhouette of Irumel in ruined temples calling for compassion."}

    {"[head=3][color=goldenrod]Theomimes («god-mimickers»)[/color][/head]"}

    A spirit that, during its development, absorbed the energy of a god or the Void and underwent evolution into a higher spirit, becoming closer to its god. It may manifest self-awareness or gain possession of other beings, becoming an evil caricature of a god — a mnemonapper. These are malicious, fanatical copies of gods. They steal the image, speech and power of a deity, distort them, and act in a name they never received. Some mnemonappers gather their own 'false-cultists', convincing them of their authenticity.

    {"[head=3][color=goldenrod]Fragments of Will[/color][/head]"}

    Appear in places where a god has previously manifested. These are fragments of past manifestations that have lost connection with their source. They are mindless, but carry the mannerisms, voice and behavioral patterns of the deity. They are 'living' memories of the god's actions at that time. They may become dangerous if they believe they must 'carry out the will' once more.
